           DIGITAL INTRODUCES StorageWorks RAID ARRAY 230


          New High Performance, High Availability PCI-Based
                   RAID Subsystem for PC Networks


MAYNARD, Mass. -- July 11, 1995 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced the StorageWorks RAID Array 230, the industry's 
fastest, most flexible storage solution for Intel PCI-based systems.  
The StorageWorks RAID Array 230, a competitively-priced RAID 
solution for PC networks, utilizes PCI bus technology to help users 
overcome system performance bottlenecks and gain rapid access to 
critical data.

     Incorporating Digital's modular StorageWorks architecture, the 
RAID Array 230 is easily expandable to meet growing capacity, 
performance and availability requirements.  Designed for Intel 
systems running Novell NetWare 3.12/4.1 or Microsoft Windows NT 
V3.5, the StorageWorks RAID Array 230 is similar in functionality to 
the EISA-SCSI-based RAID Array 210.  The subsystem allows users to 
mix and match drive types and tailor the subsystem to their specific 
performance and fault tolerance needs by providing user-selectable 
RAID levels.

     With continuous increases in server processor speeds, storage 
access has the potential to become a system bottleneck," said Paul 
Feresten, vice president of sales and marketing for Digital's 
Storage Business Unit. "By incorporating new bus standards and 
technologies, such as PCI, into RAID subsystems, we solve that problem 
by providing products that deliver fast throughput and high availability.  

     In addition, by offering both EISA and PCI backplane RAID 
controllers supported on any industry standard Intel server, 
Digital's StorageWorks products offer unmatched flexibility to meet 
a range of users' needs."

     The University of Wyoming, Cheyenne, Wyoming, an organization 
that has successfully used the StorageWorks RAID Array 210, now has 
plans to install RAID Array 230 subsystems with 48 GB of storage.  
"We acquired fifteen RAID Array 210 subsystems to provide 
uninterrupted service to users who pay to use the university's 
computing resources," stated Lynn Lawton, systems programmer.  
"Along with fault tolerance features for data reliability, the RAID 
Array 230 will provide us with even greater speed and performance, 
particularly with the PCI backplane, all at an attractive price. 
Initially equipped with 15 to 20 megabytes of storage capacity per 
student, the 230 will accommodate about one-third of our 10,000 
students.  And, if demand exceeds supply, it's easy to expand."

     "Since its introduction last July, the RAID Array 210 has 
successfully met the need for EISA-based storage solutions for the 
growing number of companies installing Windows NT, as well as for 
the large base of Novell NetWare users," said Bruce Baird, PC 
networks marketing manager for Digital's Storage Business Unit.  
"Now, with the integration of a PCI backplane, the RAID Array 230 
offers a complete, cost-effective storage solution for those who 
want the unsurpassed performance and data availability of PCI.  

     In addition, support for multiple RAID levels allows optimized 
flexibility in running the system to meet specific application and 
business needs."

StorageWorks RAID Array 230

     The StorageWorks RAID Array 230, a hardware-based disk array, 
provides expandable, cost-effective storage for RAID levels 0, 1, 
0+1, 5, and JBOD (just a bunch of disks), and supports both 8-bit 
and 16-bit drives.  With a 4MB FastCache (expandable to 32MB), the 
array is optimized for fast read-write operations.  The RAID Array 
230 provides continuous access to data via automatic, unattended 
rebuilds and hot spare/hot swap disk drive configurations.  In 
addition, the RAID Array 230 offers extended fault tolerance with 
redundant power supplies and cooling fans.

     The controller-based RAID array features throughput of up to 
2,679 I/Os per second and bandwidth of up to 17.8MBs per second.  In 
a standard configuration, the product comes equipped with three 
1.05GB or 2.1GB 16-bit wide disks with room for three additional 
disk drives in the same enclosure.  Additional performance features 
include a user-selectable write-back cache, resulting in faster 
execution time; data striping, allowing users to boost request or 
data transfer rate; and tag command queuing, allowing each drive to 
process multiple commands simultaneously.

Pricing and Availability

     The StorageWorks RAID Array 230 Subsystem is priced starting at 
$6,935 and consists of a StorageWorks PCI backplane hardware RAID 
controller, RAID configuration and management utilities software, 
redundant power supplies, and three 1.05 GB disk drives with the 
appropriate cables and enclosure. The product is available today from 
select value-added resellers (VARs), wholesale distributors and systems 
integrators.  The RAID Array 230 is backed by an industry-leading 
warranty, including a five-year warranty on disks and a three-year 
controller and packaging warranty.  For more information about Digital's 
complete line of StorageWorks products, call 1-800-STORWORK 
(1-800-786-7967).

     The StorageWorks product line is a comprehensive family of 
disk, tape, and optical storage subsystems, offering modular, 
competitively-priced, industry-standard solutions in customizable 
desktop, deskside, departmental, and datacenter enclosures.  
StorageWorks products are available on multiple operating systems 
and hardware platforms, including Digital workstations, systems and 
servers; Novell and Windows NT environments; and Hewlett-Packard, 
IBM, and Sun workstations and servers.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.
